        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "b6c0ffa9-1f96-538f-867e-b16707e73fd4",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Lower Lake Stone Jail, Sulphur Bank Mine, Eastlake, Lake County, California, Lower Lake, Lake County, California\n- Topics: quicksilver operations, mercury production, civil order, endemic plants\n- Plants: Eriogonum nervulosum\n\nFacts:\n- From 1873 to 1883, the first quicksilver operations of the Sulphur Bank Mine drove rapid town growth and created an urgent need for civil order.\n- During 1873 to 1883, the need for civil order during growth around the Sulphur Bank Mine necessitated the building of the Lower Lake Stone Jail.\n- The Sulphur Bank Mine covered 150 acres and became one of the most noted mercury producers in the world.\n- Eastlake, Lake County, California, was located just southwest of the Sulphur Bank Mine and 7 miles north-northwest of Lower Lake at an elevation of 1,355 feet (413 m).\n- Eriogonum nervulosum is an uncommon plant endemic to the inland North Coast Ranges of California.\n- Eriogonum nervulosum is known from only a handful of occurrences, most of which are in Lake County.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "416af520-f5f6-5968-9c60-d70c57955a7b",
        "text": "Page: Lower Lake Stone Jail. Sentence: During the days of the first quicksilver operations of the Sulphur Bank Mine, lasting from 1873 to 1883, rapid town growth and the urgent need for civil order necessitated the building of a jail.\nPage: Sulphur Bank Mine. Sentence: The 150 acre mine became one of the most noted mercury producers in the world.\nPage: Eastlake, Lake County, California. Sentence: It was located just southwest of the Sulphur Bank Mine 7 mi north-northwest of Lower Lake, at an elevation of 1355 feet (413 m).\nPage: Eriogonum nervulosum. Sentence: This uncommon plant is endemic to the inland North Coast Ranges of California, where it is known from only a handful of occurrences, most of which are in Lake County.",
